## 5. Results — the routed approach

**Scale:** 160 alerts, 2,409 alert-risk pairs judged, zero failed calls.

### Where the 1,229 golden links ended up

| Outcome | Count | What it means |
|---|---:|---|
| **Caught** | 259 | Model found it, golden confirms |
| Judged not linked | 839 | Model saw it and declined |
| Never judged | 131 | Model was never shown it (the routing gap) |
| Judged insufficient | 0 | |
| **Total** | **1,229** | |

**Recall = 259 / 1,229 = 21.1%**

### New links surfaced
**169** predicted links are not in the golden at all. These are candidates for expert review, not errors.

### How narrow the routing really was

This is worth highlighting to anyone assessing the design:

- Only **62 of 116** risks were ever offered to any alert
- Only **~85 of 116** risks have any Regulation Summary parent at all in the mapping data — so roughly 31 risks are unreachable by this design no matter what
- One risk (`LR-00000906`) appears in the mapping tables but has no published text, so it can be referenced but never actually judged

### A genuinely useful finding: the cost lever

We tested what would happen if we capped the number of candidate risks per alert:

| Cap at top… | Links still findable | % of achievable |
|---:|---:|---:|
| 10 | 773 | 62.9% |
| 20 | 1,056 | 85.9% |
| 30 | 1,096 | 89.2% |
| **40** | **1,098** | **89.3%** |
| 50–100 | 1,098 | 89.3% |

**It flatlines at 40.** The routed approach could be capped at 40 candidate risks per alert with *zero* loss of recall. That's a straightforward efficiency saving, available today, with no downside.

The 89.3% ceiling is not caused by the cap — it's the 131 links with no routing path at all. That's a data-mapping limitation, not a tuning choice.

